HW-026  ES8311: a COLD power-on does NOT go deaf. HW-023's explanation is refuted,
        and the root cause of the six seconds is open again.

        M5Stack StickS3 (3rd unit, COM35), Zephyr 4.4.0, 2026-07-13.
        The board was PHYSICALLY UNPLUGGED and REPLUGGED. This is the one thing no
        amount of west flash, sys_reboot() or reset-on-connect can simulate: the
        ES8311 has no reset pin, so none of those reach it.

WHAT HW-023 CONCLUDED, AND WHY IT NEEDED THIS RUN
-------------------------------------------------
HW-023 established -- correctly, and it still stands -- that the ES8311's ADC returns a
noise floor of exactly zero for about 6.2 seconds after the 0x00 block reset, and that
there is no sample-rate dependence (the rate sweep had been reading its own elapsed time
as a frequency response).

It then EXPLAINED that with a tidy story: the reset clears CSM_ON, which powers the
analog down; the ES8311 has no microphone-bias pin and its analog reference sits on three
1 uF capacitors (VMID, ADCVREF, DACVREF); discharging and recharging them is the six
seconds. The story predicts something testable and expensive: a genuine cold power-on
starts from fully discharged capacitors, so a cold-booted board's microphone should be
deaf for the same six seconds.

It is not. The story is wrong.

RESULT
------
Cold power-on. t is milliseconds since POWER WAS APPLIED, not since a reset. The sweep
reaches its first rate 2.2 s into the boot, which is the earliest this instrument can
sample anything.

    8000    t=2239 ms   floor=10053   tone_rms=1254    adc=alive   PASS
    11025   t=3708 ms   floor=176     tone_rms=2239    adc=alive   PASS
    12000   t=5078 ms   floor=152     tone_rms=1672    adc=alive   PASS
    16000   t=6181 ms   floor=298     tone_rms=2942    adc=alive   PASS
    22050   t=7047 ms   floor=101     tone_rms=3976    adc=alive   PASS
    24000   t=7862 ms   floor=88      tone_rms=4001    adc=alive   PASS
    32000   t=8541 ms   floor=108     tone_rms=5221    adc=alive   PASS
    44100   t=9105 ms   floor=99      tone_rms=10179   adc=alive   PASS
    48000   t=9642 ms   floor=117     tone_rms=14533   adc=alive   PASS

All nine rates pass. The ADC is ALIVE at 2.2 seconds after power-on.

What it does show is a settling transient of a completely different shape: the noise floor
starts about a hundred times too high (10053 against a settled 81..116) and DECAYS to
baseline by roughly seven seconds. That is a large analog offset being nulled out, not a
dead converter.

THE THREE CASES, AT THE SAME MOMENT
-----------------------------------
    at t = 2 to 3 seconds          noise floor
    -------------------------------------------------------------------
    cold power-on (this run)       10053, decaying   ADC ALIVE
    warm boot (control, measured    93, flat         ADC ALIVE
      many times today)
    after a 0x00 reset (HW-023)     0                ADC DEAF

Three different behaviours at the same elapsed time, so this is not an artefact of when
the instrument happens to look. The reset and the cold boot are genuinely different
events, and only the reset kills the ADC.

WHAT THIS MEANS
---------------
1. HW-023's MEASUREMENT stands: the 0x00 reset costs about 6.2 seconds of a zero noise
   floor. Reproduced many times.

2. HW-023's EXPLANATION is refuted. A cold power-on charges the same three capacitors
   from zero and does not go deaf. So the discharge is not the mechanism, or not all of
   it. Root cause is open again.

3. The conclusion "do not reset this part" is STRONGER, not weaker. The reset costs
   something a cold boot does not. It is not "the cold start you were going to pay
   anyway, brought forward" -- it puts the part somewhere a cold start never goes.

4. One thing is worth noting even though it explains nothing yet: cycling only the
   codec's ANALOG supply (the L3B rail, M5PM1 PYG2) reproduces the deafness exactly, with
   no reset at all -- while cutting power to the WHOLE BOARD does not. What (a) the 0x00
   reset and (b) the L3B cycle share, and (c) the cold boot does not, is that they are
   PARTIAL power-downs. That is a hypothesis. It has not been tested and it is not a
   finding.

AND THE HONEST NOTE
-------------------
The claim this run refutes was mine, it was three hours old, and it was already committed
-- in the driver's comments, in the devicetree binding, and in the risk register -- as
"what it cannot avoid is the same six seconds on a genuine cold power-on". It was a good
story that fit every measurement I had. It took one physical unplug to kill it, and the
only reason it did not ship is that somebody pulled the cable.

METHOD, AND WHY IT IS AWKWARD
-----------------------------
A cold boot cannot be captured the usual way. The COM port does not exist until the board
is powered, so a monitor cannot be attached before power-on; by the time it is, the first
rates have already been measured and printed into a void. And the monitor must attach with
--no-reset, because a reset-on-connect would not re-discharge anything -- that is the whole
point -- but it WOULD restart the sweep, which would then run warm and measure nothing.

So each row is stamped with the milliseconds since boot, stored, and the table is replayed
on an endless loop afterwards. A monitor attached minutes later still reads the run. An
earlier version replayed only twenty times and the window was missed.

